Here are the three most effective ways to implement this, ranging from simple to advanced.

---

### Approach 1: The "State Machine" (Best for Linear Stories)
This is the most straightforward method. You define a current state (e.g., "Intro"), and based on the user's choice, you transition to the next state.

**Pros:** Easy to read, easy to debug, good for short scripts.
**Cons:** Can become messy if the story branches heavily (spaghetti code).

#### Implementation
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class DialogueSystem {
    private Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    private String currentState = "start";

    public void run() {
        while (currentState != null) {
            currentState = processState(currentState);
        }
    }

    private String processState(String state) {
        switch (state) {
            case "start":
                System.out.println("NPC: Hello, traveler! What is your name?");
                System.out.println("1. I am a hero.");
                System.out.println("2. I am a thief.");

                System.out.print("Choose (1 or 2): ");
                int choice = scanner.nextInt();

                if (choice == 1) return "hero_path";
                if (choice == 2) return "thief_path";
                return "start"; // Retry if invalid
            case "hero_path":
                System.out.println("NPC: A hero! I will help you.");
                return "end";
            case "thief_path":
                System.out.println("NPC: A thief? I will report you.");
                return "end";
            case "end":
                System.out.println("Game Over.");
                return null; // Stop the loop
            default:
                System.out.println("Unknown state.");
                return null;
        }
    }
}
```

