Overloading vs Overriding

Overloading and Overriding are two important concepts in object-oriented programming. They are often confused with each other, but they serve different purposes and have different characteristics.

Overloading

Overloading is the ability to define multiple methods with the same name but different parameters in a class. The parameters can differ in type, number, or order. The method that is called depends on the arguments passed to it.

For example, in Java, you can have the following methods in a class:

public void print(int x) {
System.out.println("Integer: " + x);
}

public void print(double x) {
System.out.println("Double: " + x);
}

public void print(String x) {
System.out.println("String: " + x);
}

In this example, the print method is overloaded with three different parameter types. When you call print(5), the first method is called. When you call print(3.14), the second method is called. When you call print("Hello"), the third method is called.

